---
// User docs, written by hand. The nightly developer-docs job only writes
// src/content/devdocs/, so it never changes this page.
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Messaging"
  description="Chat with OMAR and review and run saved workflows from Slack."
>
  <p class="docs-lead">
    Connect OMAR to Slack to chat with the assistant and to review and run your
    saved workflows from a DM or a channel thread.
  </p>
  <pre><code>omar messaging slack connect</code></pre>
  <h2>Prerequisites</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Linux or WSL.</li>
    <li>
      <code>omar</code> and <code>omarc</code>, tmux, and a signed-in agent backend.
    </li>
    <li>
      A system font for graph previews, for example <code>fonts-dejavu-core</code>
      on Ubuntu.
    </li>
    <li>
      For source builds, build the compiler with
      <code>(cd lang &amp;&amp; lake build Omar.Verify omarc)</code> before building
      OMAR.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Create the Slack app</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Create your app from
      <a
        href="https://github.com/omar-os/omar/blob/main/integrations/slack/app-manifest.json"
        ><code>integrations/slack/app-manifest.json</code></a
      >.
    </li>
    <li>Enable Socket Mode and install the app to your workspace.</li>
    <li>Create an app-level token with <code>connections:write</code>.</li>
  </ol>
  <h2>Connect</h2>
  <p>
    On first use, <code>omar messaging slack connect</code> asks for provider
    credentials and approved user IDs, saves them privately, and starts or
    reuses the local runtime at <code>127.0.0.1:45073</code>. OMAR selects and
    remembers the assistant chat automatically. Keep the connector running;
    rerun the same command to reconnect.
  </p>
  <h2>Use it in Slack</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>In Slack</th><th>Behavior</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>/omar help</code></td><td>Show the commands.</td></tr>
      <tr
        ><td><code>/omar list</code></td><td
          >List workflows approved and saved in OMAR.</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td><code>/omar run NAME</code></td><td>Run a saved workflow.</td></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td><code>/omar new-chat</code></td><td
          >Start a new conversation with the assistant.</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td>A regular DM</td><td>Goes to the assistant.</td></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td><code>@Omar</code> in an approved channel</td><td
          >Ask a question or send a control; replies stay in the originating
          thread.</td
        ></tr
      >
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Use the slash commands in a DM. Slack slash commands cannot be invoked
    inside threads, so use mentions there.
  </p>
  <h2>Review a workflow</h2>
  <p>
    Review a proposed workflow's graph and source files, then choose
    <strong>Run workflow</strong>, <strong>Revise</strong>, or
    <strong>Cancel</strong>. Only the requester can approve that version.
  </p>
  <h2>Security</h2>
  <p>
    Treat approved users as trusted operators: they can direct agents using the
    host's files, tools and configured harness credentials. Separate chats do
    not isolate files or persistent agent memory.
  </p>
  <p>
    Never distribute a shared bot token to untrusted users; it permits bot
    impersonation and access to the bot's permitted traffic. Saved tokens use
    private Unix file permissions, not encryption.
  </p>
  <h2>Log out</h2>
  <pre><code>omar messaging slack logout</code></pre>
  <p>
    Removes saved credentials. Logout does not revoke provider tokens, clear
    environment variables, or stop a running connector.
  </p>
  <h2>Limitations</h2>
  <p>
    Queues, event deduplication and busy state are in memory. Restarting a
    connector during a run can lose result delivery; there is no durable outbox.
    Starting a new chat resets the conversation, not harness memory. Each
    connector process has its own queue.
  </p>
  <p>
    Run <code>omar messaging slack --help</code> for provider requirements.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>

<style>
  .docs-lead {
    color: var(--text-bright);
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    line-height: 1.75;
  }
</style>
